WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:06.700
- Hi there.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.200
I'm Seth Juarez and this is
the Microsoft Research Faculty Summit 2017,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.840
where the theme is
The Edge of AI.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.180
And I've got a special guest to talk
about a really interesting project.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.220
So let's talk a little bit about the
actual problem that you're working on,

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.760
because I saw a demo of this

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.240
and I was absolutely floored
by how amazing it was.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.140
Why don't you give us a little
background of what it is this does?

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.760
- Yeah, sure.
So first, you know,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.540
that cancer is one of the biggest killers,
right, in the world

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.920
and the incidents
of cancer are just going up.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.880
As people get older in the world,
more and more people get cancer eventually.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.000
So one of the few ways to treat
cancer is radiation therapy.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.440
And about half of cancer
patients get radiation therapy.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.240
Radiation therapy is about using
a special purpose-built machine

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.800
that delivers
ionizing radiation

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.960
to a specific part of your body
where the tumor is,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.740
or a patient's body
where the tumor is,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.840
and with the intent
to kill the tumor cells

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.240
or at least to subside their growth.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.820
And this radiation therapy,
the planning of this treatment,

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.120
is powered by medical imaging.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.860
So what they do essentially,
they take a scan, a CT scan or

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.140
MRI scan and they need
to basically outline

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.880
every organ that there is there,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.420
to tell the machine

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.760
what exactly needs to be treated.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.820
And I have a little video here,
so we can look at it.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.180
Here is a radiation oncologist planning
a treatment of a patient.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.200
So you can see that
it's a very primitive tool.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.760
You just draw outlines, right,
and then you switch to the next slide

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.400
and you draw another outline,
so you know,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.140
a medical image is multiple slices
taken one after another.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.460
And after like 300 slices,
or 200 slices, it's a very manual,

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.820
very laborious process.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.220
And we've built a tool that helps save time,
that takes this process from between 30

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.180
and 40 minutes down to five.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.380
- And this is interesting, right,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.440
because when you're shooting
this radiation at the tumor,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.980
you don't want to hit anything else,
because it's doing harm to the tumors,

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.780
you don't want to do harm
to the other things.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.480
- Exactly, exactly.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.840
That's why you don't only have to,
you know, delineate the tumor.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.100
You also want to delineate
all the organs around the tumor.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:13.040
And in the end, you have to delineate
like up to 20, 30 different structures in one image.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.280
And some of the structures, like tumors,
they are different between patients,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.440
but some of them
are the same.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.720
So in the end, the radiation oncologist
ends up comparing hundreds of the same hip bones

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.560
that look exactly the same
between patients,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.160
but they still have
to do that.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.900
- And that's 40 minutes of work
for the radiation oncologist.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.480
Is this the actual doctor that's doing this?

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.520
- Yeah, it depends on the case, right.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.620
So brain tumors, they might take longer,
like prostate, some are simpler.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.940
In some centers, the radiation oncologist,
a highly-paid physician is doing that.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.820
In some centers they have people
whose job is to do this.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:54.140
But yes, it's an expensive thing
to do, and usually it's physicians who do that,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.240
instead of you know,
using the time with the patient.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.680
- To actually treat the patient.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.380
I mean 40 minutes of treating the patient

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.140
is a lot different
than effectively doing Photoshop

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.140
on tons of slices of a diagram
of what's inside the body, right?

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.340
- Yeah.
- So what does InnerEye actually do to help that,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.120
and how does AI help?

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.500
- So basically our team,
there is a team of researchers in our

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.540
MSR Cambridge UK lab who worked
on computer vision

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.780
and image segmentation problems.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.800
For many, many years they contributed
to Microsoft Kinect,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.560
and right now we are using
the same principles

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.060
to build machine-learning

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.320
models that can actually detect,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.940
that can actually find those body
parts for you in an image.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.780
So this is our tool, this is the project
that we're working on,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.660
and it looks like any other
medical imaging tool.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.440
So you see all the standard components,
the three cardinal planes.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.260
We have this 3-D render in here.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.600
But this has this neat little button
that says auto-structure set

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.360
And this is something that none of the tools
in the market currently have.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.520
And you can see that it allows you
to select what kind of image are we looking at,

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.600
and what kind of structures
are we looking at in this image?

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.620
In this case we have a computer tomography
scan of a patient with prostate cancer open here.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.400
And when I press the start button
it kicks off the evaluation

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.980
of our machine-learning model,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.200
which has been trained
on hundreds of images

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.140
from participating institutions

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.460
and hospitals
that we work with.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.380
It has seen a lot of those contour tests,
and in just under a minute, probably,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.780
here you have the pretty
accurate segmentation

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.100
of a prostate cancer case.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.220
And this is not 100 percent correct.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.860
So some physicians will now look
at that and say that, OK,

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.080
so I don't like how this
bladder is coming out here.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.500
So the yellow portion
here is bladder.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:44.360
So probably click the edit button
and I would kind of fix it up a little bit.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.920
But you know, still, rather than
doing it from scratch, you're just fixing up the work.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.300
The majority of the work,
90 percent is done by AI.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.380
- So you've taken something that may
have taken 40 minutes to an hour down

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.280
to like maybe five or six minutes
to make sure that it's OK?

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:00.960
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.500
This is the hope, right.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.280
And again, you're fixing someone else's work
as opposed to like creating the same thing

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.540
over and over again.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:10.060
- So this is pretty amazing.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.340
And it looks like you're able to also map
into the 3D picture as well.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.920
Tell us about what we're looking at here.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:17.520
- Exactly.
This is 3D render,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.380
this is another kind of product
that came out of our

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.620
MSR Cambridge Lab.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:30.600
It kind of renders the data sets in three dimensions
and you can actually look at the organs

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.140
and you can see how well it scanned.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.800
- And that's what it's actually
figured out from the pictures.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.160
- Yeah, that's what it figured out from the pictures.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.340
So you can see the right hip bone, for example,
here that was contoured by the automated algorithm.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.060
If you know much about anatomy,
you see that it's pretty much accurate.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:50.800
- So let's talk about the AI
that was involved in making this actually work,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.040
because it feels a little magical.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.220
But I want to like, we're all,
this is the faculty summit.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.260
We're talking about research.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.500
- Absolutely.
- How did this happen?

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:05.160
You mentioned a little bit about sort of the AI
that was happening at Cambridge, like the Kinect stuff.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.000
Did some of that, was some
of that involved with this?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.740
Because it feels like the same kind of problem.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.240
-Yeah.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:16.300
You know, so the Kinect project started way back
when, in the early 2000s, I think.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:21.500
And what they have done for Kinect,
they were using machine-learning

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.960
models to solve an image segmentation problem.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.480
- Right.
- So they were using three dimensional data.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:32.080
They were using the flat image from the Kinect camera,
plus using the depth information from the depth sensor

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.180
to find body parts in the image.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.900
To tell that this voxel in this image
probably belong to the left arm,

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.340
this is the right arm, this is the head
and these are the legs.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.880
So in machine-learning speak,
this is a classification problem

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.260
that were on the classification
algorithm on each voxel.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:53.020
So the same guys who worked on this,
and my boss Antonio Criminisi was a principal researcher on this project,

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.760
he and I contributed to Kinect.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.720
So they took this idea further.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.920
This idea is based on
the concept of decision trees.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.780
The algorithm has been improved significantly.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.660
- Sure.
- It has been tuned to fit

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.880
this particular problem
of medical image segmentation,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.220
but the core
is kind of the same.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.280
So you have this decision tree, and the idea
of the decision tree is that each node,

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.660
you train a very simple classifier

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:19.540
that tries to maximize kind of
the purity of the classes.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.920
So all the training points,
all the voxels, get into one node

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.160
and then you try to figure out
to split them into parts

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.300
so that the voxels

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.280
that probably belong
to one kind of type

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.860
of data
goes to the left node

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.520
and another goes
to the right node.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.020
And the process happens over and over and over,
and here down at the tree you end up

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.360
with having to split

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:43.900
between like a voxel

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.000
that more likely belonged
to a bladder or a voxel

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.600
that more likely belonged
to a prostate.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.980
And at the very end, you have
very pure classes, this is how decisions...

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.580
- So when I remember, I remember
the Kinect was certainly novel

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.760
because I mean here,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.840
I understand what decision trees are and usually I'm more about,

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.900
well what's the feature selection
that you used.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.180
In the Kinect, they actually picked the point
that you want to predict

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.000
and picked the same set
of points around it,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.040
not just with the actual image,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.780
but with the actual depth data,
and those were the features.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.000
Is it similar in this case?

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:12.860
- Yes.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.680
So the feature selection process here
is somewhat similar to Kinect.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.420
So in Kinect what they used was,
they were looking at the current voxel

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:22.120
and they were randomly sampling
a few points around this,

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.920
right, in a 3-D cube around this voxel.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:28.140
So what we're doing here, we tried that
and we found right now I think

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.720
we're using just the full-fledged
3-D convolutions.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.080
We're looking at all the voxels around them,
and kind of assigning weights to them,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.780
the tree is assigning weights to them.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.300
And we've taken this process
a little bit further.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.760
So as opposed to doing this just based
on the voxels around the current voxel,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.420
we also used cascades of decision trees.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:51.340
And in reality, the decision forest, many to one,
but the output of one forest serves

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.960
as an input into another forest.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.340
So the forest downstream looks at the voxels,
you know, the voxel intensities,

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.560
but also looks at the predictions
from the previous forest.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:05.100
So it knows that, you know, this voxel to the left
is something that looks like a right femur,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:08.340
to the right is something
that looks like a left femur.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.860
And so probably this voxel
is more likely to be a bladder,

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.020
because it's between something
that looks like hips.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.700
- So let's talk about this,
because this feels very similar to maybe something

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.960
that would happen in
a neural network but more treelike.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.400
Is it similar, different?

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.400
- Yeah, you can say so.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.400
So you know, these days everybody
talks about deep learning, right?

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.400
So what deep learning really is,
you bring multiple layers of network

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.920
and layers can do different things a little bit,

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.260
but you use the output of one layer
as an input into another layer.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:40.680
And in the end you end up with kind
of a layer that is something,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.940
uses high level features as an input,
input tree, right?

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.680
Like at the beginning you might have been
looking at just pixel voxel intensities,

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.020
and the layers further to the end
you're looking at high-level things

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:52.520
like a nose,
you know,

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.400
if you're trying to classify
a human face like nose,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.120
ears, eyes, things like that.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:01.520
So something similar is happening here, we're
just not using neural nets, we're using decision trees.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.240
- But they're nested.
- There's multiple.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.620
Yeah, they're like cascades
of those decision forests.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.580
- So let's talk a little bit
about this program that you've built.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.920
Is this, did you use the word tomography,
did I get that right?

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.840
Is it a tomography-type program?

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.800
- So tomography is a method of imaging the human body.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.560
- Right.
- So there are like machines

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.800
that use certain physical kind of phenomena

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.660
to get an imaging
of the human internals.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.220
There are several
kinds of tomography.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.100
There is computer tomography,
there's some medical resonance tomography.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:39.520
So our algorithm right now for the prostate
scans works on computer tomography,

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.900
but really kind of the underlying
technique can work on any kind of image.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.420
- See, and that was the question because
what I was leading to is,

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.140
tell me about the dataset that you use

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.480
in order to train this effectively?

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.780
Because I'm assuming that,
what was the size of your dataset, for example?

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:54.960
And then, what kind of data?

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.240
Because it looks like
we focused on prostate cancer.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.780
Will this work to extrapolate other organs
inside of the body?

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:03.500
- Yes.
So actually working

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.420
with the dataset ended up being more challenging

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.100
and much more interesting
than we thought it would be,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.780
because there is so much variation

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.820
in how prostate cancer

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:13.960
patients are imaged
between different institutions.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:15.820
You don't think, you know,
just take a computer tomography,

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:19.260
everyone looks like kind of
the same and go for it.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:19.900
Well no.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.960
So one institution likes to image patients
with contrast inside their bladder;

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.140
another institution would implant
something in there.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.780
Somebody else will do some
additional contraptions that would,

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:34.020
you know, that would fixate
the patient in one place.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.820
So a lot of effort went into generalizing
between the different ways,

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.100
how different institutions
kind of image their patients.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:47.020
But the underlying technique,
like I said, it's a classification problem

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:48.960
that looks at the individual voxels.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.400
It doesn't really care whether it's
a computer tomography or a medical resonance tomography

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.480
or whether it's a functional

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.520
MRI or a PET scan.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:04.100
So it will work with any kind of 3-D
image data and moreover, so that's one of the benefits.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:07.340
One of the reasons why are
using decision trees as opposed to like neural nets,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.060
it can use multiple input layers.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:15.340
Because like for brain
cancer patients, they usually take different kinds of

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.560
MR scans with different settings.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:22.580
Like there's T-1 gab with contrast injection,
then there is a flair that looks at,

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.360
and they are looking
at different aspects,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.640
the different clinical
characteristics of the tumor.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:31.820
And usually a physician looks
at multiple of those scans.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:35.120
So this kind of algorithm
can use multiple channels, actually.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:39.700
It can look at multiple kinds of images
and it can kind of predict based on that.

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:44.000
But what's interesting is if you,
for example, trained it to look on three kinds of

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.500
MR scans but you only have one
at the time of scan,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.740
it would still work because of the kind
of nature of these.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:52.040
- So were you using the three
different kinds of scans?

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.940
And the reason why I'm asking is because this is
super interesting in terms of the data

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.080
you were using
and the algorithms you are using.

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:00.180
Were you using all three
of the scans to do the training?

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.860
But because it had all three scans,
it inferred well enough to be able

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:05.240
to just use one of them?

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.300
- Yes.
Because of the decision tree, right?

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:12.300
If you don't have, and just for clarity,
so what I just showed, the prostate case,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.280
we only used one type of scan,
we don't use the multi-layer,

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:16.980
but this is something
that has been done in the research.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:18.540
But theoretically, yeah.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.420
This technique, if you trained it on three layers,
like three types of MRI, but you only have one,

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:23.980
it'll just use the part of the three

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.240
that you know that has to do
with this one MRI.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:27.280
- I see.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.440
- So the other branches of the tree just won't be used,
voxels won't go there.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:37.100
- What if you have one of these kinds of scans,
and you don't, for example,

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:39.140
because it looks like you had
a specific prostate set.

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:40.760
You might have a set of other sets.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:42.220
What if you just want to go in and color?

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:46.060
Because I remember, like in Photoshop
there is this like magic wand tool

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.140
to like select the right area.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.900
Is there a way to do that
with this program as well?

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.880
Do you get what I'm asking?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:56.940
So this program, we wanted to build this program,
we didn't want to build, you know,

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.200
an abstract kind of research tool

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.960
that only does one thing well,

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.640
which is not practical at all.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:07.060
We wanted to build something practical,
and we built in a lot of more conventional tools

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:08.340
that people would use.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.860
And I can show you here, like a quick demo.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:15.860
For example, this is a patient, this a different
patient, so this is not a prostate scan anymore.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:20.940
This is a scan of the thorax,
of your chest parts and this is a patient,

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.880
this patient has been injected
with contrast dye,

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:27.420
so you can see this kind of huge
white spot in the middle.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.400
This is the aorta
with a contrast injected in it.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:35.640
And let me try to contour the aorta here,
so you can see I'm using kind of

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:38.080
a very natural tool, this pen.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:44.320
And then I draw a contour
around this slice.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:48.900
And normally the radiation oncologist
would switch to the next slice.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.020
- For every slice he would have to draw.
- Yeah, for every slice.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.600
So I won't do that.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.360
I will just skip through,
skip over a few hundred slices

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:57.520
and go to the point
where the aorta splits.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:02.360
So you can see the two white spots here,
but trust me, this is the place

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.180
where the aorta splits into left
and right femoral arteries.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.540
So let me draw the contour around those.

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:10.760
All right.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:16.440
And you can see that right now,
the algorithm had run an interpolation,

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:21.920
our interpolation algorithm has run
and it has contoured the aorta.

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:25.860
You can see it's not like 100 percent precise,
it captured some of the vertebrae here.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:32.620
So I can click there, all right,
and I can again use my pen to fix this slice up.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:38.720
And I'll just delete all this vertebrae artifacts,
and probably part of the kidney that it grabbed here.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:43.680
And just now, the algorithm was again
developed in our Cambridge lab,

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.960
is trying the interpolation
in three dimensions

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.180
across what like
208 slices here.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:53.880
And we have a pretty accurate segmentation
of the main blood vessel in this patient.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:57.160
So we have tools like that,
which allow the physicians

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:59.060
to do the entire

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:01.960
contouring work flow within a tool.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:05.260
- And that's quite remarkable,
because there may be a situation

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:06.860
where there is a type of cancer

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:09.980
that hasn't been trained on,
where you can go ahead

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:13.060
and do this kind of technique
inside of these scans.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:14.200
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.760
And in the end, right,
we don't say that this technology

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:20.000
is supposed to replace kind of the radiation oncologist
and do all their jobs.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.200
- Just a fast start.
- Yeah, it's a fast start.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.520
And in many cases they would want
to contour more things, you know.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:29.080
Or here we have the seven organs,
like bladder, femurs, prostate.

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.660
But probably they will contour
another like five or 10.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:37.000
They would want to do several kind
of subtle volumes around the tumor itself,

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.960
because they want to treat a little bit
of a larger region than the tumor.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:45.020
And the actual tumor they might want
to contour like in a portion of the liver

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:46.460
that gets into there
or something like that.

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:50.360
- And this is using the same machine learning techniques
from before, but to do extrapolation.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:51.880
Is that right?
- No.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:56.380
What I was just talking about
is they will use the simpler tools like the ones

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.240
that I've shown you now,
to do some of this interpolation

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:02.040
to contour the organs
that machine learning had not seen.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.400
- I see, I see.
- Right, so the algorithm that we use,

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:06.700
technically it's not machine learning.

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:08.820
- Yeah, it feels like it would be like
a search of some kind.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:12.000
- Actually it is solving the memorization problem.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.380
So in a sense, it is machine learning,
but it runs locally.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.240
There is not like string test phase.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:20.300
- I see.
- It just optimizes kind of the path between.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:24.400
- And I mean, it's pretty amazing
because now you're taking someone

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:27.240
that's having obviously
a very bad time there, they have cancer,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.960
and you're minimizing the amount of useless work

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:32.640
that a trained physician or someone

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.140
in the medical community has to do,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.420
and they could focus now
on the care of the actual patient.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:37.240
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:41.220
This is the promise here, because we believe,
we call this Assistive AI,

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.000
as opposed to, you know,

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:44.780
AI that's kind of
replacing a radiologist.

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:49.780
Because right now, there is this kind of buzz in this,
especially in the medical community that AI is coming,

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:51.260
it's going to replace a radiologist,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:52.680
radiologists will go out of a job.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:55.700
So people are really apprehensive, you know.

00:17:55.700 --> 00:18:01.820
They are scared of this advent of AI,
and we think that it doesn't have to be like that,

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:05.120
because first, humans are
pretty good at diagnosing.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.180
So right now there are a lot of start-ups
and a lot of companies out there working

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:09.420
on developing an AI

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:12.440
that will kind of diagnose cancer
based on medical imaging.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:16.200
Well it might be useful, but really,
you know an experienced radiologist

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.600
will diagnose cancer based on the medical
imaging in a few seconds.

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.360
They don't need to really have AI for that.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:21.800
- So this is amazing stuff.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:24.240
Just to finish up, what's next for Project InnerEye?

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:28.180
- There are plenty of things that this
kind of technology can unlock.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.580
There is this whole field
of quantitative radiology.

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:35.920
And surprisingly, this whole kind of medical
imaging area has been under-served by AI.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.440
There aren't really good tools
that help physicians become better

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.640
and help them
spend more time with patients.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:45.040
So you know, you can track
tumor progression over time.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:51.180
You can do things like adaptive radiation therapy,
where an AI system would actually look at

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:55.000
how a tumor responds to the radiation treatment
in real time between sessions,

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.600
and would adjust the treatment
based on that.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.160
Because right now, they build one plan,
the one that I've shown you, and they use that plan,

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.580
pretty much unchanged through
multiple sessions of therapy, right.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:06.280
And the tumor obviously changes.

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:09.720
So you know, a lot of things
that we can do to improve the outcomes.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:10.980
- So this is amazing stuff.

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:15.360
And the thing I like about it most
is that we can do a lot with computer vision.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:18.200
We can match images and we can sort images, you know?

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:22.640
This is probably one of the best applications
of computer vision that I've seen,

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:26.500
because I mean, it goes to the heart
of our humanity in curing cancer.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.580
Thanks so much for spending
some time with us, Ivan.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:30.280
- Thanks for having me.
- Thanks so much for watching.

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:31.800
We will see you next time. Take care.

